  "text": "A type of coarse-grained inorganic ion‑exchange material was made by simultaneously precipitating the active exchange components together with an inert filler. The resulting granules could be easily shaped into packed columns that offered low resistance to fluid flow. Their performance was tested by measuring how well they captured a radioactive cesium isotope, a common alkaline earth metal, and a radioactive thorium isotope from acidic solutions. The exchange phase consisted of a mixed precipitate formed from a phosphate‑based compound and a related phosphorous compound, combined with a silica‑derived substance. In acidic media the silica component did not significantly alter the behavior of the active phases, making these mixed precipitates useful both for fundamental studies of new inorganic ion exchangers and for practical radiochemical separations."
